# OS Unveiled
Context switch managed by hardware. It is very dependent on the hardware. As in, the hardware defines "context" and what it consists of. 

Rings describe user and kernel mode. Lower numbers is closer to kernel mode. Trap is an instruction that queries the trap handler table. 

There are privileged registers and a Current Privilege Level register.

The page table base register is called `CR3`. `CR2` is the address of the instruction accessing memory.

A page table entry has a present bit to indicate whether the page is in memory (`1`) or SWAP (`0`). If it's in SWAP, it points to where. If it's in SWAP, a page fault is sent for the hypervisor to handle. The physical host address may change. 



# Virtual Machines
At some point, chip frequency hit a power wall that melted. Therefore, multi-core replaced faster chips.

Dark Silicon keeps part of the chip off to manage heat and not melt anything. This is facilitated with accelerators that specialize in certain tasks (e.g., an accelerator for encryption and decryption) that stay off when the task isn't needed at the moment. 

GPUs' high bandwidth memory (HBM) does have page tables that can map from VPA to HPA.

<!-- probably when the OS expects a certain GPU -->
The cycle is app, GPU instantion, GPU drivers, and the hardware GPU. Emulating the hardware is too difficult, so API remoting at the GPU instanution level is used. So when the VM makes a CUDA call, it is intercepted and transformed accordingly and sent to the GPU. This is slow but robust.

The VM doesn't handle errors, but rather the KVM. 



# Isolation
The 0-day attack targets a vulnerability that can gain access to a machine in less than a day.



# Containers
For maximum security, the container(s) are run on a VM. To keep the benefits of containers, the VM is actually a micro-VM.

Running a container for a different OS than the host OS requires a VM of that different OS. Docker and other container platforms do that for you.

Memory and scheduling have to go to the container layer, and the host OS just sees it as the container, not the processes inside the container. 

<!-- stuff below this is techniques before containers. idk what to do with this -->
`chroot` achieves file system isolation. It makes `/` refer to the argument. Therefore, it is not possible to see what's before the argument; only after. However, `mount` is still an available call. 

Free BSD offers a lot of the isolation like containers. A process can be put in this jail, and it and all its descendants will be in this jail. `mount` is constrained to the jail this time. However, the OS has to check every time if the process is in jail or not. This metadata is part of the PCB.

`/proc` contains the files for file system configuration. For example, `/proc/<pid>/ns` contains the namespace number of <pid>. This can be used to determine whether two processes are in the same namespace.

Containers are stateless thanks to the private layer being temporary. In other words, containers do not depend on being in a particular state. Being stateless boosts scalability because it reduces the amount of setup necessary.

User-space instructions on a VM or container have the same performance as running the instruction directly. System calls also has no calls since either architecture has to interface with the OS anyway. Privileged kernel instructions, however, have a cost on VMs only since it goes beyond the guest OS.



# Unikernels
Exokernels proposed library OS's to be compiled with the application. These library OS's then invoked the exokernel. The problem is that the library OS needs to be in the same language as the application in order to be compiled with it. This can get out of hand pretty quickly.

Hypervisors already execute instructions in the right ISA and handles I/O. Therefore, unikernel developers only need to ensure the unikernel works on the few kernels that exist. This is substantially less targets than ensuring they work on all hardware. 

The ideas of exokernels are realized with unikernels (and libraries compiled into the image) and a hypervisor.

<!-- platform layer -->
Unikernels expect to interact with a hypervisor like KVM or XEN. That way, the backends of devices are abstracted away. So when a device updates or a new device is added, it doesn't matter to the unikernel because the frontend doesn't change. The backend does, though, but that is the hypervisor's responsibility. 

Interacts in the OS primitives layer is minimized in order to avoid the interdependent subsystems issue

Unikraft compiles the source code as normal then links it with the custom libraries. This allows unikernels to be smaller than containers. They are also more secure since they do not rely on the host OS. 